  <h1>Turtle Graphics</h1>
  <div class="alert alert-info alert-dismissible fade show">
    <p>
      Turtle graphics is a popular way for introducing programming to non-programmers.
      More information about turtle graphics on <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turtle_graphics">Wikipedia</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is a simple <strong>turtle graphics implementation</strong> in JavaScript that works in browser.
      Imagine a robotic turtle starting at (0, 0) in the x-y plane.
      Give it the command <code>D(50)</code>, and it moves (on-screen) 50 pixels in the direction it is facing, drawing a line as it moves.
      Give it the command <code>+(45)</code>, and it rotates in-place 45 degrees counter-clockwise.
      There are many other commands as well as variables, conditions, loops and procedures.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Development:</strong>
      from 2021.
      Technologies: JavaScript (ES6), Rollup.js, jQuery, Bootstrap 5, CodeMirror.
      Simple object-oriented design.
      The source code is hosted on <a href="https://github.com/Hartrik/turtle-js">GitHub</a>.
    </p>
    <button type="button" class="btn-close" data-bs-dismiss="alert" aria-label="Close"></button>
  </div>

